1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now it's the old scout master

2
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:11,480
Well, we were out camping this past weekend had a lovely time

3
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:15,680
Weather was good one of the scouts bought a boomerang and

4
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:18,840
He asked me if I knew how to throw one and

5
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:24,360
You know, I couldn't remember but then it came back to me

6
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:30,040
Hey, this is podcast number 69

7
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:56,720
You

8
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:06,039
Welcome back to the scout master podcast, this is Clark Green. Let's take a look in the mail bag

9
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:10,759
Here's one from Nick Demos scout master troop 42 in Clifton Park, New York

10
00:01:10,759 --> 00:01:15,600
Nick had this to say I've been a scout master for a year and I'm in the middle of the daunting task of changing

11
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:21,360
From being adult lead to being boy lead using the patrol method at times

12
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:27,280
It's been very difficult and your podcast has been a wealth of knowledge and help. Thank you. Well, thanks, Nick

13
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:31,240
Thanks for the kind words Nick shared this story in his email

14
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:34,520
He said a while back you discussed how hard it would be to teach a scout

15
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:37,200
10 knots in one sitting

16
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:42,080
Compared to teaching them one knot when they really needed it this past weekend

17
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:48,039
My troop was moving brushes part of a service project a new scout asked me to help him tie a rope to a piece of

18
00:01:48,039 --> 00:01:50,280
Brush so he could drag it instead of having to carry it

19
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:55,240
I showed him the timber hitch using the edge method and he learned how to tie that knot in minutes

20
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:59,680
I saw him tie the timber hitch three or four more times for three or four more trips to the brush pile

21
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:04,400
Thanks so much for that pearl of wisdom and I'm so glad I had the opportunity to test it and see it work

22
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:08,479
Keep up the good work some days. I really need help with all those scouts and

23
00:02:08,479 --> 00:02:13,160
Scouters and parents who think that there are better ways to run the troop instead of using the patrol method

24
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:18,080
Yeah, there might be simpler and more efficient ways to run a troop of Boy Scouts than the patrol method

25
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:21,800
But when we try and make things simple and efficient

26
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:27,920
Well, sometimes we end up with something that's not actually scouting. So keep up the good work, Nick

27
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:30,120
I'm sure things are going to work out just fine

28
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:33,720
Jeff Swartt who is a committee co-chair of troop

29
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:37,680
347 in Logan, Ohio wrote in and said once again

30
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:42,480
I want to commend you for your great service to scouting your podcast and website are great resources

31
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:46,840
I recommend your works to all my scouting colleagues even my 18 year old ventureer

32
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:51,960
Who used to be a Boy Scout likes them? Well, I'm glad to hear that Jeff

33
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:55,680
Jeff also gave me some technical information about the mp3

34
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:57,520
file and

35
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:02,200
The size of the files that I end up publishing is the podcast and I'm going to do my best to follow

36
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:06,400
Some of your advice there Jeff. I really appreciate it. I'm not a I'm

37
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:12,680
Nowhere near being a recording expert or anything like that and I really appreciate the information that y'all send in

38
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:19,920
So if you see a way for the podcast or the website to be improved. Hey, don't hesitate. I can certainly use the help

39
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:25,320
Mike Manzer wrote in he's a scout master from troop

40
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:30,000
469 in Kate in Georgia and he had this to say about our last podcast

41
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:35,840
Wow, what incredible timing the interview with Alec Kossoff could not have come at a more appropriate time

42
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:41,680
our troop is planning for an 80 mile backpacking trip that covers the Georgia section of the Appalachian Trail at

43
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:48,120
The end of this month your interview with Alex really served to reinforce the importance of advanced planning and

44
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:53,240
I especially enjoyed the discussion about how teens deal with stress and challenge

45
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:58,000
I've never really given this much thought until the opportunity pops up, which they always do

46
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:04,120
The most important part of the discussion for me was setting expectations for emotional issues before they ever happen

47
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:09,400
Thanks for the great work and with the podcast keep them coming. Well. Thank you Mike

48
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:16,320
I certainly appreciate the kind words once again, and if you haven't listened to podcast number 68

49
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:24,440
That was an interview with Alex Kossoff who is the author of the Appalachian Mountain Club guide to outdoor leadership

50
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:29,400
you can find a review of that book on the blog at

51
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:38,360
Scoutmastercg.com and if you're planning one of these outdoor adventures or hey, you know just weekend camping trips and things like that

52
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:44,280
There's a lot of very useful information in the guide to outdoor leadership. So this time around

53
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:51,040
We're going to answer an email question and then I have a little bit of a story to tell that's right

54
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:55,960
Now some of the story is ideas from my personal experience

55
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:59,520
Personal experience with some of my friends and colleagues in scouting

56
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:03,040
Some of the things that I've heard and seen a little poetic license

57
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:08,840
But I got to thinking about a few things over the past several weeks and I wrote them down in a story form

58
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:14,600
I hope you enjoy them and that sounds like enough for any podcast, doesn't it? Well, let's get started shall we?

59
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:25,480
Email that is folks and here's an answer to one of your emails

60
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:38,760
Listener who wish to stay anonymous writes in to say I love your podcast and I have for a long time

61
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:43,720
I have a question. I'd appreciate your insight because you're really dialed in and you call them as you see them

62
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:49,240
I've recently been nominated to serve as our district commissioner not having filled this role

63
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:56,440
Before I'm anxious to do a good job. Have you any advice for me anything? I should focus on from your perspective

64
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:58,920
What makes a good district commissioner?

65
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:05,400
Well, I got to tell you I've never served as a unit commissioner. I've never served as a district commissioner

66
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:09,920
I've worked with plenty of them and I've served as a camp commissioner for two or three years

67
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:15,640
So my advice is kind of rough and ready shoot from the hip on the district commissioner job

68
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:21,200
And I think it would apply in a lot of ways to many district level jobs

69
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:23,480
And I have hold held a couple of those over time

70
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:26,560
number one

71
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:34,440
Demystify everything that you do really few unit level volunteers know what a commissioner is or what they do and in my opinion

72
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:38,240
We seriously need to look into changing the name commissioner

73
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:41,840
What is a commissioner? I don't know

74
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:51,120
I mean when we encounter somebody with a title commissioner it intends some things of official dumb and judgment and

75
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:56,040
It sounds kind of police like and I don't know whether I care for the

76
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:00,160
So I would tell everybody that maybe your mission is to cooperate

77
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:06,400
With them to deliver the scouting program your mission is to co-operate co-missioner

78
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:11,240
I mean maybe maybe that would help and maybe that would make everybody a bit more comfortable

79
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:17,640
And then give them three bullet points about what you do that is relevant to their position as a volunteer number two

80
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:19,640
I would be incredibly useful

81
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:21,920
That's what I would suggest

82
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:27,560
What do I mean? Well, maybe you can tote around the current copies of all the forms

83
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:32,040
that are most commonly needed by people in volunteer positions and

84
00:07:32,440 --> 00:07:35,400
And a big list of resources that people can find online

85
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:40,280
And I would have as many answers at my fingertips as I possibly could

86
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:45,680
If you can convince the folks at your council scout shop to give you a few copies of common reference books

87
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:51,880
You know like the scout master handbook the troop committee guide the guide to safe scouting cub program books the advancement

88
00:07:51,880 --> 00:07:57,440
Guide the guide to safe that you can throw in the trunk of your car and have instantly ready for sale when you visit your units

89
00:07:57,600 --> 00:08:03,560
That might be a big help to if you can carry a collection of position related patches

90
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:10,680
So much the better be incredibly helpful be somebody that when they when you walk through the door at a unit meeting

91
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:15,280
They're very very happy to see you and they know they're going to get their questions answered

92
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:18,000
And they know that you're going to have the resources to help them out

93
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:20,960
now number three

94
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:22,960
I would clear out the deadwood and

95
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:26,400
I forgive me if that term sounds a little harsh

96
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:30,520
But I would go to a district meeting and I would say as of this year

97
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:36,040
We have a three-year term limit on commissioners in our district and those who have served for three or more years

98
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:40,600
Presently will have a date that will set next year to find and train their replacement

99
00:08:40,920 --> 00:08:45,440
now why am I saying this while I learned this from somebody who I know who

100
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:48,520
started out as a unit leader and then became a

101
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:54,560
Leader in his district and then a leader in our council and then moved on to regional and national jobs

102
00:08:55,480 --> 00:08:59,160
and has been lauded and awarded for all of them and

103
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:05,000
His philosophy is very simple. His rule of thumb is very simple the first year. You're in the position

104
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
You're learning how to do the position

105
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:12,120
The second year you're being as useful as you possibly can in that position in the third year you are

106
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:14,760
naming and training your

107
00:09:15,319 --> 00:09:16,720
successor and

108
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:22,200
This really does keep things rolling. It keeps new talent new ideas and new people in in the process

109
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:26,960
My experience with commissioners is they usually sign on in groups

110
00:09:27,960 --> 00:09:29,600
especially in a district

111
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:35,640
That group hangs out for four or five years and then they're all done and then the district kind of did

112
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:38,360
There's around for a year or two trying to locate the next group

113
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:43,640
and you know the the one or two guys that hang on and

114
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:48,960
End up being commissioners for a decade. Maybe really never should have been commissioners in the first place

115
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:55,080
It's just been my experience. We're much more likely to be successful in recruiting commissioners

116
00:09:55,080 --> 00:09:58,120
If their career is mapped out as a three-year commitment

117
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,199
Naturally, we'll come up with a brief and meaningful

118
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:07,240
Investiture and retirement ceremony for round tables and we'll just keep things rolling

119
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:13,560
We'll keep like I said the number of people and ideas turning over so that the job has always looked at a

120
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:18,199
Fresh number four is I'd have short targeted and useful meetings every other month

121
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:21,199
Now I know districts are in the habit of meeting once a month

122
00:10:21,199 --> 00:10:24,680
I know that commissioners are in the habit of meeting once a month

123
00:10:24,680 --> 00:10:29,319
But I have to question whether once a month is too frequent for there to be actual

124
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:32,080
Actually any value to the meeting

125
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:35,560
Every other month. There's going to be plenty of new things to look at

126
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:41,040
I would limit that meeting to one hour and I really mean one hour with a set agenda and

127
00:10:41,719 --> 00:10:46,199
Everybody with a very solid expectation of how much time they're going to have to talk at the meeting

128
00:10:46,199 --> 00:10:48,319
And then we turn off the lights and lock the doors

129
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:52,240
If everybody wants to stand outside in the parking lot for another hour or two

130
00:10:52,240 --> 00:10:52,719
That's fine

131
00:10:52,719 --> 00:10:59,880
But one hour at the meeting table and that's it and that will respect people's time keep them action oriented and

132
00:11:00,480 --> 00:11:04,319
I think it can also solve an awful lot of difficulties that arise

133
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,040
in districts number five I would track unit visits and

134
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:16,959
Reviews pretty religiously. I would want a minimum of a once a quarter visit from commissioners with their units

135
00:11:16,959 --> 00:11:18,800
So that would be four visits a year

136
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:21,680
I know a lot of commissioners can manage more than that

137
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:27,000
But at least one of those visits would be to a unit meeting one would be to the unit committee meeting

138
00:11:27,199 --> 00:11:33,520
One to the patrol leaders council meeting in the case of troops or to the officers meeting in the case of crews

139
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:36,959
Or to a couple of random den meetings in the case of packs

140
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:43,400
The fourth visit would be a visit to a unit event that was held outside the meeting place

141
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:45,760
Maybe in a cub pack

142
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:47,760
It's a tour or a visit of some kind

143
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:52,320
And you know in a cub in a scout troop or a venture crew it might be a camp out

144
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:55,840
But I would want to see the unit function in many different ways

145
00:11:56,760 --> 00:12:03,120
And then every visit gets a report one side of the report is the roses side and that means

146
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:05,440
What's going well?

147
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:11,440
Congratulations atta boys things you've seen that are very positive whatever and the other side of that report is thorns

148
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:17,480
What the unit needs to work on either things they have asked for or things that you've observed and the rule of thumb

149
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:21,720
You know maybe five roses to two thorns in other words

150
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:25,880
We're really emphasizing the positive things that units are doing now

151
00:12:25,880 --> 00:12:30,320
the roses side of the page goes on the district website and it gets emailed to unit leaders and

152
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:37,080
To the officers of the sponsoring institution. Did you hear that to the officers of the sponsoring institution?

153
00:12:37,080 --> 00:12:41,440
they very rarely hear anything about what the unit is doing and

154
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:46,760
Pasted telephone polls whatever we can do to sing the phrases of any volunteer

155
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:52,800
The thorns side is turned into action items for immediate attention and only sees the light of day between

156
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:58,800
The commissioners in the district the thorns all die in 60 days

157
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:01,600
They've been addressed and resolved and

158
00:13:02,640 --> 00:13:07,880
Roses are everlasting. We're always repeating the praises of those volunteers

159
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:11,000
Number six. I would take the pledge

160
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:18,040
Here's the pledge as a commissioner. I will never ever ever on pain of dismissal do any of the following a

161
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:22,480
Point out an incorrect badge placement to everyone ever at all I

162
00:13:22,480 --> 00:13:30,200
Will not speak of uniforming or the placement of badges or g-jaws or metals or ribbons on the uniform

163
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:33,280
Unless answering a direct question from someone

164
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:41,280
B. I will never ever discuss any units difficulties or shortcomings with any other volunteer or parent in another unit

165
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,720
anyone at all outside of the district commissioner meeting and

166
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:51,200
Especially if their spouse if the commissioner spouse is a volunteer

167
00:13:51,560 --> 00:13:53,560
That is especially important

168
00:13:54,120 --> 00:14:01,660
See I will never ever deliver any messages in a form for anyone other than informational announcements from the district or the council

169
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:09,920
I'm not going to deliver messages between volunteers between parents and volunteers between professionals and volunteers district staff and volunteers

170
00:14:10,280 --> 00:14:14,200
When requested to do so I can respond with the contact information

171
00:14:14,800 --> 00:14:19,360
For whom the message is intended and this especially applies to forwarding emails

172
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
D. I will never ever

173
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:29,680
Instruct a volunteer and or a scout in how to do something from tying a knot to running a meeting unless I am asked and

174
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:33,800
Only after the questioner has gone to considerable trouble to drag it out of me

175
00:14:35,080 --> 00:14:38,400
E. I will never ever complain about procedure

176
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:41,040
district or professional staff

177
00:14:41,040 --> 00:14:47,480
volunteers scouts or the general state of affairs in the community nation or world and will never tell anyone how things were

178
00:14:47,480 --> 00:14:51,800
Different and better when I was a scout master cup master scout or a youngster

179
00:14:52,680 --> 00:15:01,720
F. I will never ever be anything less than non-sectarian in matters of politics custom and religion while serving in my capacity as a commissioner

180
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:03,520
now

181
00:15:03,520 --> 00:15:08,520
That's a very targeted pledge and it would remove 80% of the content of

182
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:14,040
Commissioner speech and visits and actions that cause problems rather than solve them

183
00:15:14,439 --> 00:15:21,760
So that is my like I said kind of shoot from the hip stab at what would make a good unit commissioner

184
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:28,040
And what would make a good district commissioner my experience in the field is limited your mileage may vary

185
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:31,920
And I would love to hear your ideas so you could contact me at

186
00:15:32,839 --> 00:15:36,560
scoutmastercg at Verizon.net

187
00:15:44,040 --> 00:16:04,819
If I knew then what I know now I would have done things differently now how many times have you caught yourself saying that?

188
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:07,160
it's never the case but

189
00:16:07,640 --> 00:16:09,640
It's a pretty common thought

190
00:16:10,120 --> 00:16:14,640
Especially when you hit one of those big birthdays where you get the funny birthday cards about how old you are and

191
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:17,640
It can set you back on your heels a bit

192
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,300
And it could also get you to remembering now

193
00:16:21,800 --> 00:16:26,319
You know we tell our scouts that that thing about scouting being a trail

194
00:16:26,880 --> 00:16:28,880
with landmarks along the way and

195
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:34,400
You know, I think sometimes we may miss the idea that it's a trail for us, too

196
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,960
That we're learning and growing right beside them

197
00:16:38,800 --> 00:16:44,480
And more importantly, we're getting a look at something that a lot of parents don't get to see

198
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:52,160
We're getting an uncommonly close look at the way that they're growing and maturing as we go through scouts together

199
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,440
Now I went looking for something the other day and

200
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:02,880
Other than thinking that we really needed to clean out a lot of stuff out of the house

201
00:17:04,040 --> 00:17:07,680
I knew that if I looked hard enough I would find it

202
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:13,319
And here it is. This is the uniform shirt that he wore when he was in Cub Scouts

203
00:17:25,960 --> 00:17:31,800
When he turned six he was off to the first grade and he said his new friends were going to be in something called tiger cubs

204
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:34,880
Now that sounded like fun. I had a vague

205
00:17:35,240 --> 00:17:39,340
Remembrance of being in Cub Scouts when I was a boy. I remember having fun

206
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:43,520
But I didn't remember that there was anything called tiger cubs. I

207
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:46,560
guess it was something new and

208
00:17:47,400 --> 00:17:52,360
I do remember hoping that they still did those pinewood car things, you know

209
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:59,360
So we ended up at a meeting and they really needed people to help out. Of course, I raised my hand

210
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:04,840
Without having any idea what I was getting myself into and their eyes really lit up

211
00:18:04,880 --> 00:18:07,120
When they heard that I was in scouts when I was a kid

212
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:13,420
Of course, it turned out we weren't just helping we were running the whole show

213
00:18:16,640 --> 00:18:20,840
Eight six year olds in your basement is a pretty intense experience

214
00:18:20,840 --> 00:18:26,480
I mean you can give them a soda straw and some scotch tape and a paperclip and they'll amuse themselves for hours

215
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:33,320
But you know, I did read the book and I did some searching and I took a couple of training sessions

216
00:18:33,320 --> 00:18:35,320
and that helped a lot and

